+++# ADR-0001: Tool-call risk gating is enforced host-side, not by an in-sandbox hook
+++
+++Status: accepted
+++Spec: `.keel/specs/tool-call-risk-gate/`
+++
+++## Context
+++
+++A spike proposed flagging risky agent tool calls with a local decision
+++model, invoked from a "PreToolUse"-style hook on the agent CLI (e.g.
+++Claude Code's own hook mechanism), which would call out to a Moor decision
+++daemon before letting a tool call proceed.
+++
+++Claude Code runs *inside* the sandbox container (`docs/THREAT-MODEL.md`,
+++"What moor is protecting"). A hook registered with it is agent-side
+++configuration that also lives inside the sandbox, under the same account
+++the agent itself controls. `docs/THREAT-MODEL.md` already reasoned through
+++this exact shape of control for a different case — a `pre-push` git hook
+++baked into the base image — and rejected it:
+++
+++> a git hook runs *inside* the sandbox, under the same account that
+++> controls its own git config — the agent (or a cloned repo's
+++> `.gitattributes`/`core.hooksPath` override) can simply reconfigure or
+++> bypass it, so it would be a control that looks like a boundary but isn't
+++> one.
+++
+++Nothing about a `PreToolUse` hook changes that reasoning. It runs as agent
+++configuration, in the same untrusted execution context as the tool calls
+++it would be gating. An agent (or a prompt-injected instruction reaching
+++it) that can edit its own hook config, or that finds one path the harness
+++doesn't route through a hook, defeats the control at zero cost.
+++
+++`moor run <project> -- <cmd>` (`cli/src/commands/run_cmd.rs`) is, today,
+++the one place a command actually crosses from the host process into
+++`docker exec`. It execs unconditionally and only records what happened
+++afterward — there is no gate there at all yet, deterministic or
+++otherwise. `moor keel` (`cli/src/commands/keel_cmd.rs`) builds its argv
+++and calls the same function, so anything gated there covers both.
+++
+++## Decision
+++
+++Risk verdicts are enforced host-side, in `run_cmd::run`, before it builds
+++the `docker exec` argv. This is the same trust boundary the existing
+++push-detection and audit logging already rely on: the host process is
+++trusted, the sandbox is not, and only something outside the sandbox can
+++be a real boundary rather than a suggestion.
+++
+++Concretely:
+++
+++- `cli/src/decision.rs` exposes a pure `evaluate(cmd) -> Verdict`, checked
+++  against a fixed, in-process rule table. `Allow` or `Deny { rule_id,
+++  reason }` — no `Ask`, no model, yet.
+++- `run_cmd::run` calls it before doing anything Docker-related. `Deny`
+++  appends a `decision` entry to the project's audit chain (rule id, the
+++  redacted command) and returns before `docker exec` is invoked at all.
+++  `Allow` changes nothing: the existing exec/push path is untouched.
+++- An in-sandbox hook is not ruled out for later, but it is *signal*, at
+++  the same trust tier as keel's own sink payloads (`docs/THREAT-MODEL.md`,
+++  "The posture attestation and keel's sink"): recorded faithfully, never
+++  treated as proof, and never the thing that actually blocks execution.
+++- A future decision model plugs in behind `decision::evaluate`'s call
+++  site, not behind a new in-sandbox interception point. Swapping the rule
+++  table for a model-backed `DecisionModel` trait should not require moving
+++  where enforcement happens.
+++
+++## Consequences
+++
+++- Coverage is exactly what already crosses `moor run`/`moor keel`. It does
+++  not see commands typed inside an interactive `moor shell` session, or
+++  calls the sandboxed MCP server dispatches internally — the same
+++  limitation the existing push-detection comment already documents for
+++  itself.
+++- `Deny` is the only verdict this ADR commits to. An `ask` verdict needs a
+++  human-in-the-loop surface that does not exist yet and is deliberately
+++  out of scope for this spec.
+++- The rule table starts small and deterministic (credential-path reads,
+++  Docker-socket/`sudo` references, recursive wipes of `/`, `/workspace`,
+++  or `~`) so every verdict is testable and auditable without a model in
+++  the loop. A model backend is future work, not blocked by this ADR, but
+++  not introduced by it either.
